{{Politics of Malawi}}

Presidential elections were held in [[Malawi]] on 23 June 2020, having originally been scheduled for 19 May and later 2 July.<ref>{{cite news |title=Not May 19, Malawi fresh elections slated for July 2-MEC |url=https://www.nyasatimes.com/not-may-19-malawi-fresh-elections-slated-for-july-2-mec/ |accessdate=24 March 2020 |publisher=Nyasa Times |date=2010-03-24}}</ref><ref name=MN/> They followed the annulment of the results of the [[2019 Malawian general election|2019 presidential elections]], in which [[Peter Mutharika]] of the [[Democratic Progressive Party (Malawi)|Democratic Progressive Party]] had received the most votes.

The result of the re-run elections was a victory for [[Lazarus Chakwera]] of the [[Malawi Congress Party]], who defeated Mutharika by  margin of 59% to 40%.

The elections were hailed by observers as being both free and fair.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2020/6/27/malawi-presidential-election-lazarus-chakwera-declared-winner|title=Malawi presidential election: Lazarus Chakwera declared winner|website=Al Jazeera|date=27 June 2020}}</ref>

==Background==
In the [[2019 Malawian general election|May 2019 general elections]], incumbent President [[Peter Mutharika]] of the [[Democratic Progressive Party (Malawi)|Democratic Progressive Party]] (DPP) was re-elected with 39% of the vote, defeating [[Lazarus Chakwera]] of the [[Malawi Congress Party]] (35%) and [[Saulos Chilima]] of the  United Transformation Movement (20%). The DPP also remained the largest party in the National Assembly, winning 62 of the 193 seats. However, the presidential election was challenged in court by Chakwera and Chilima, and on 3 February 2020 the [[Constitutional court|Constitutional Court]] annulled the presidential election results, citing evidence of irregularities, and ordered fresh elections be held within 150 days.<ref name=AJ>{{cite web|url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2020/02/malawi-top-court-annuls-presidential-election-results-200203060112731.html|title=Malawi top court annuls presidential election results|publisher=Al Jazeera|last1=Chitete|first1=Suzgo|date=4 February 2020}}</ref>

Parliament passed the [[Parliamentary system|Parliamentary]] and [[Presidential election|Presidential Elections]] Act (PPEA) Amendment Bill on 24 February, setting 19 May as the date for the new presidential elections and extending the terms of MPs and local councillors by one year to allow for harmonised presidential, parliamentary and local elections in 2025.<ref name=MN>{{cite web|url=https://mwnation.com/elections-may-19/|title=Elections May 19|publisher=The Nation Malawi|last1=Nyale|first1=Enelless|date=25 February 2020|access-date=25 February 2020|archive-date=25 February 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200225215253/https://mwnation.com/elections-may-19/|url-status=dead}}</ref> In March the Malawi Electoral Commission announced a new election date, 2 July, one day before the 150-day limit to hold elections set by the Constitutional Court.<ref>{{cite news |title=2020 presidential elections to be held on July 2 |url=https://malawi24.com/2020/03/23/2020-presidential-elections-to-be-held-on-july-2/ |accessdate=24 March 2020 |publisher=Malawi24 |date=2010-03-23}}</ref> On 21 May the Legal Affairs committee of parliament endorsed fresh presidential elections to be held on 23 June rather than 2 July.<ref>{{cite news |title=June 23 for Malawi fresh presidential election endorsed by Parliamentary Committee |url=https://www.nyasatimes.com/june-23-for-malawi-fresh-presidential-election-endorsed-by-parliamentary-committee/ |accessdate=22 May 2020 |publisher=Nyasa Times |date=21 May 2020}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
As a result of the Constitutional Court ruling, the [[President of Malawi]] would be elected using the [[two-round system]],<ref name=AJ/> replacing the former [[first-past-the-post voting|first-past-the-post]] system used in 2019.<ref>[http://www.electionguide.org/elections/id/2320/ Republic of Malawi: Election for President] IFES</ref>

==Candidates==
Lazarus Chakwera and Peter Kuwani filed their respective nominations on 6 May 2020. Peter Mutharika filed his nomination the following day, with Atupele Muluzi as his running mate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Malawi president files for election rerun with ex-leader's son |publisher=[[Al Jazeera English]] |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2020/05/malawi-president-files-election-rerun-leader-son-200507170549462.html |date=7 May 2020}}</ref>

==Opinion polls==
A poll conducted by IPOR Malawi showed that 53% of respondents expected Lazarus Chakwera to win the election, while 31% expected Mutharika to win.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Matonga |first1=Golden |title=Opposition are favourites on eve of Malawi's presidential election |url=https://mg.co.za/africa/2020-06-22-opposition-are-favourites-on-eve-of-malawis-presidential-election/ |accessdate=29 June 2020 |publisher=Mail & Guardian |date=22 June 2020}}</ref>

On a national level, according to the polls, 51% would vote for Chakwera, while 33% for Mutharika and 0.2% for Peter Kuwani.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://mobile.twitter.com/ipormalawi?lang=en|title=IPOR Malawi (@IPORMalawi) on Twitter|website=mobile.twitter.com}}</ref>

Another poll by [[Afrobarometer]] suggested that Chakwera was most likely to win the election.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://allafrica.com/stories/202005180560.html|title=Malawi: Chakwera Leads in Malawi Opinion Polls - MCP Says Not Taking Anything for Granted|first=Wanga|last=Gwede|date=May 18, 2020|website=allAfrica.com}}</ref>

